{"name":"Money Tree","hex":"#c9937a","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"money tree","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An imaginary tree from which money can be plucked. See money doesn't grow on trees.","The Malabar chestnut, Pachira aquatica."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/money_tree"}],"entries":[{"title":"Money tree (myth)","blocks":[{"text":"Chinese legend has it that the money tree is a type of holy tree that can bring money and fortune to the people, and that it is a symbol of affluence, nobility and auspiciousness. It can be traced back to primitive societies when the adoration of a holy tree was prevalent. Whilst Money trees may be derived from the Sun tree myth associated with paradise, the coins link paradise with a material bounty in this world. According to the existing historical narratives, the concept of the \"money tree\" is derived at the latest from the Han dynasty. Cast-bronze money trees are a conspicuous feature of Han tombs in Sichuan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Han_Money_Tree.JPG/330px-Han_Money_Tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Money_tree_(myth)","wikidata":"Q6899308"},{"title":"Money","blocks":[{"text":"Money is any item or verifiable record that is generally accepted as payment for goods and services and repayment of debts, such as taxes, in a particular country or socio-economic context. The primary functions which distinguish money are: medium of exchange, a unit of account, a store of value and sometimes, a standard of deferred payment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Euro_coins_and_banknotes_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Euro_coins_and_banknotes_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Money","wikidata":"Q1368"},{"title":"Tree","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable for timber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree","wikidata":"Q10884"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Han_Money_Tree.JPG/500px-Han_Money_Tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Han_Money_Tree.JPG/1280px-Han_Money_Tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Han_Money_Tree.JPG/500px-Han_Money_Tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Han dynasty money tree stored in the Hong Kong Heritage Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHan_Money_Tree.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Modern_money_tree.jpg/500px-Modern_money_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Modern_money_tree.jpg/500px-Modern_money_tree.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A modern 'money tree' observed in Yunnan, China, 1 December 2015","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AModern_money_tree.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Euro_coins_and_banknotes_%28cropped%29.jpg/5